"78":{
"id":"78",
"head":"王",
"e":"天下所歸往也。董仲舒曰：“古之造文者，三畫而連其中謂之王。三者，天、地、人也，而參通之者王也。”孔子曰：“一貫三爲王。”凡王之屬皆从王。[李陽冰曰：“中畫近上。王者，則天之義。”]",
"p":"wánɡ",
"r":"王",
"v":"01",
"w":"𠙻",
"variants":[
{
"head":"𠙻",
"e":"古文王。"
}
],
"indexes":[
"王",
"𠙻"
],
"rid":"sw:78",
"eEn":"The one to whom all under Heaven turn and go. Dong Zhongshu says: “When the ancients created writing, they drew three strokes and connected their centers, calling it 王. The three are Heaven, Earth, and humanity; the one who joins and connects the three is the king.” Confucius says: “One stroke running through three makes 王.” All characters in the 王 class are formed with 王. [Li Yangbing says: “The middle stroke is close to the upper one. Being king carries the meaning of modeling oneself on Heaven.”]",
"translationStatus":"retained-exact"
},
"79":{
"id":"79",
"head":"閏",
"e":"餘分之月，五歲再閏，告朔之禮，天子居宗廟，閏月居門中。从王在門中。《周禮》曰：“閏月，王居門中，終月也。”",
"p":"rùn",
"r":"王",
"v":"01",
"w":"",
"variants":[],
"indexes":[
"闰",
"閏"
],
"rid":"sw:78",
"eEn":"A month arising from the accumulated remainder of the calendar, with two intercalations in five years. At the rite of announcing the new moon, the Son of Heaven occupies the ancestral temple; during an intercalary month, he occupies the space within the gate. Formed from 王 situated within 門. The Rites of Zhou says: “During an intercalary month, the king occupies the space within the gate for the whole month.”",
"translationStatus":"retained-exact"
},
"80":{
"id":"80",
"head":"皇",
"e":"大也。从自。自，始也。始皇者，三皇，大君也。自，讀若鼻，今俗以始生子爲鼻子。",
"p":"huánɡ",
"r":"王",
"v":"01",
"w":"",
"variants":[],
"indexes":[
"皇"
],
"rid":"sw:78",
"eEn":"Great. Formed from 自. 自 means beginning. The first sovereigns, the Three Sovereigns, were great rulers. 自 is pronounced like 鼻; in present common usage, a firstborn child is called 鼻子.",
"translationStatus":"retained-exact"
},
"81":{
"id":"81",
"head":"玉",
"e":"石之美。有五德：潤澤以溫，仁之方也；䚡理自外，可以知中，義之方也；其聲舒揚，尃以遠聞，智之方也；不橈而折，勇之方也；銳廉而不技，絜之方也。象三玉之連。|，其貫也。凡玉之屬皆从玉。[陽冰曰：“三畫正均如貫玉也。”]",
"p":"yù",
"r":"玉",
"v":"01",
"w":"𤣪",
"variants":[
{
"head":"𤣪",
"e":"古文玉。"
}
],
"indexes":[
"玉",
"𤣪"
],
"rid":"sw:81",
"eEn":"Beautiful stone. It has five virtues: its moist luster and warmth exemplify benevolence; its markings can be examined from the outside to reveal the inside, exemplifying righteousness; its sound is expansive and clear, spreading so that it can be heard far away, exemplifying wisdom; it breaks rather than bends, exemplifying courage; its edges are sharp yet do not injure, exemplifying purity. It depicts three pieces of jade linked together. The vertical stroke | is the cord passing through them. All characters in the 玉 class are formed with 玉. [Yangbing says: “The three horizontal strokes are evenly aligned, like pieces of jade strung together.”]",
"translationStatus":"retained-exact"
},
